.TH HOUSTONKVM 8 2026-10-02 "HoustonKVM" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
HoustonKVM \- IP-KVM server: drive real machines from a web browser
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B HoustonKVM
.RB [ \-\-port=\fIN\fR ]
.RB [ \-\-bind=\fIADDR\fR ]
.RB [ \-\-db=\fIPATH\fR ]
.RB [ \-\-secure\-cookies ]
.RB [ \-\-trusted\-proxy=\fIADDR\fR ]...
.RB [ \-\-allowed\-origin=\fIURL\fR ]...
.RB [ \-\-audit\-retention\-days=\fIN\fR ]
.RB [ \-\-ice\-server=\fIURL\fR | none ]...
.RB [ \-\-tls=on | off ]
.RB [ \-\-https\-port=\fIN\fR ]
.RB [ \-\-tls\-cert=\fIFILE\fR " " \-\-tls\-key=\fIFILE\fR ]
.br
.B HoustonKVM
.BR \-\-version " | " \-\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B HoustonKVM
shows the screens of machines wired to this one through capture devices, and
sends them keyboard and mouse input through a USB HID gadget, a CH9329 serial
adapter, or a QEMU monitor socket. Users sign in to its web interface or call
its API.
.PP
Installed from the package, it runs as the
.B houstonkvm
service (user
.BR houstonkvm ),
with its options in
.IR /etc/houstonkvm/houstonkvm.conf .
.PP
Only the options below are set on the command line. Everything else is
configured at runtime by an Owner in the web interface (Admin) or through the
API, kept in the database, and takes effect without a restart: targets
(capture device, resolution and frame rate, input adapter, video bitrate,
name, group and tags), users and their roles, API tokens, STUN and TURN
servers, and HTTPS. An option given here that the web interface could also
set fixes that setting: Admin then shows it read-only, and the API refuses to
change it.
.SS First run
With no accounts in the database, the server prints a one-time
.B setup code
to its log
.RB ( "journalctl \-u houstonkvm" ).
Open
.BI http:// server :8080/
and create the first Owner account with it.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-port= N
The plain HTTP port. Default: 8080.
.TP
.BI \-\-bind= ADDR
The address to listen on, for HTTP and HTTPS. Default: 0.0.0.0, every
interface. Use 127.0.0.1 when a reverse proxy on the same machine is the only
client.
.TP
.BI \-\-db= PATH
The SQLite database. Default:
.I houstonkvm.db
in the working directory; the service uses
.IR /var/lib/houstonkvm/houstonkvm.db .
Certificates installed through the web interface are kept in
.I tls/
next to it.
.TP
.B \-\-secure\-cookies
Marks the plain HTTP side's session cookie Secure. Only for a reverse proxy
that terminates TLS in front of the server: otherwise browsers silently refuse
the cookie. Not needed for the server's own HTTPS, whose cookie is always
Secure.
.TP
.BI \-\-trusted\-proxy= ADDR
A proxy whose
.B X\-Forwarded\-For
header is believed, so sign-in rate limiting counts each visitor's own
address. Repeat it for several.
.TP
.BI \-\-allowed\-origin= URL
A page origin, such as
.IR https://kvm.example.com ,
allowed to use the session cookie. Only needed behind a proxy that doesn't
pass the browser's
.B Host
header through. Repeat it for several.
.TP
.BI \-\-audit\-retention\-days= N
How long the audit log is kept. Default: 90.
.TP
.BR \-\-ice\-server= \fIURL\fR | none
A STUN or TURN server for low-latency (WebRTC) video:
.BI stun: HOST\fR[\fB: PORT\fR]
or
.BI turn: USER : PASS @ HOST\fR[\fB: PORT\fR].
Repeat it for several;
.B none
for none. Without this option an Owner sets the list under Admin \(-> Network,
and it starts empty: on a LAN or VPN the server's own addresses are all a
browser needs, and the server makes no connections out by itself.
.TP
.BR \-\-tls=on | off
Fixes whether HTTPS is on. Without it, an Owner turns HTTPS on and off under
Admin \(-> Network, and the server starts on plain HTTP.
.RS
.TP
.B on
HTTPS always. The plain HTTP port redirects to it, unless an Owner turned
that off. The server won't start without a certificate: one in
.IR /etc/houstonkvm/tls/ ,
one named by
.BR \-\-tls\-cert ,
or one an Owner installed earlier.
.TP
.B off
Plain HTTP only. Use it behind a reverse proxy that does TLS, and to get back
in after an HTTPS mistake (see
.BR RECOVERY ):
it also turns off the Owner's HTTPS, which stays off without the option.
.RE
.TP
.BI \-\-https\-port= N
Fixes the HTTPS port. Without it, it is an Owner's setting, 8443 to start.
.TP
.BI \-\-tls\-cert= FILE " \-\-tls\-key=" FILE
A certificate chain (PEM, the server's certificate first) and its private key
(PEM, not encrypted) to serve, given together. Without them, the files in
.I /etc/houstonkvm/tls/
are used when both exist. Either way, an Owner can't replace this certificate
in the web interface, and the server refuses to start when it can't be
served. The key must be RSA of 2048 bits or more, or ECDSA P-256 or P-384.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Prints the version and exits.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Prints a summary of the options and exits.
.SH HTTPS
Turning HTTPS on in the web interface needs no restart and can't lock the
Owner out: HTTPS starts beside plain HTTP, the browser is sent to the HTTPS
address, and only a browser that gets there confirms the switch. Unconfirmed
within two minutes, the server goes back to plain HTTP by itself and Admin
says why. Usually the port is blocked; see
.BR FIREWALL .
.PP
Under HTTPS everyone signs in once more: the session cookie becomes
.BR __Host\-session ,
and one that crossed the network in the clear isn't honoured.
.PP
An Owner gets a certificate in one of three ways under Admin \(-> Network:
.IP \(bu 2
From the organization's certificate authority: the server makes a key, which
never leaves it, and a signing request (CSR); the Owner pastes back the signed
certificate.
.IP \(bu 2
By uploading a certificate and its key, for example one the organization's CA
issued elsewhere.
.IP \(bu 2
From HoustonKVM's own small certificate authority, made on first use (valid
10 years). Trust its certificate once (any signed-in user can download it,
for a browser, a GPO or Ansible), and the server certificates it issues
renew themselves 30 days before they expire without anyone noticing.
.PP
TLS goes through the system's OpenSSL, so the system crypto policy
.RB ( update\-crypto\-policies (8))
decides protocol versions and ciphers; TLS 1.2 is the least it allows. Keys
are made with OpenSSL's EVP interface, which works in FIPS mode.
.SH RECOVERY
If HTTPS was turned on and nobody can reach it (the certificate is refused,
the port is closed), add
.B \-\-tls=off
to
.B HOUSTONKVM_OPTS
in
.IR /etc/houstonkvm/houstonkvm.conf ,
and run
.BR "systemctl restart houstonkvm" .
That turns HTTPS off, and it stays off once the option is removed again (and
the service restarted), so an Owner can sign in over plain HTTP, fix things
under Admin \(-> Network, and turn HTTPS on again there.
.SH FIREWALL
The package ships a firewalld service,
.BR houstonkvm ,
covering 8080/tcp (HTTP), 8443/tcp (HTTPS) and 50000\(en50100/udp (WebRTC
video), and opens nothing by itself:
.PP
.RS
.nf
firewall\-cmd \-\-permanent \-\-add\-service=houstonkvm
firewall\-cmd \-\-reload
.fi
.RE
.PP
With other ports, open those instead. If SELinux refuses a port, allow it with
.BI "semanage port \-a \-t http_port_t \-p tcp " N\fR.
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGHUP
Re-reads the certificate files: the configuration's, or the Owner's.
New connections get the new certificate; open ones, with their video and
control, carry on. A pair that doesn't load leaves the one in use in place,
and the failure is logged and audited.
.B systemctl reload houstonkvm
sends it.
.TP
.BR SIGTERM ", " SIGINT
Stop the server. Anyone driving a target is recorded in the audit log as
stopped by the server.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/houstonkvm/houstonkvm.conf
The service's options, as
.BR HOUSTONKVM_OPTS .
.TP
.I /etc/houstonkvm/tls/houstonkvm.crt
.TQ
.I /etc/houstonkvm/tls/houstonkvm.key
A certificate managed by the configuration (Ansible, Puppet, certbot): the
chain, and its key. The directory is root:houstonkvm, mode 0750; give the
key the same owner and mode 0640.
.TP
.I /var/lib/houstonkvm/houstonkvm.db
Accounts, sessions, targets, settings and the audit log.
.TP
.I /var/lib/houstonkvm/tls/
Certificates managed in the web interface:
.I cert.pem
and
.I key.pem
in use,
.I ca/
for HoustonKVM's own certificate authority, and
.I pending/
for a key waiting for its signed certificate. Back up
.I ca/
to keep a trusted CA across a reinstall.
.TP
.I /usr/share/doc/houstonkvm/examples/
An Ansible playbook for rolling out HTTPS, and certbot deploy hooks.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B HOUSTONKVM_RTC_LOG
libdatachannel's log level, such as
.BR debug ,
for troubleshooting low-latency video.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 after a clean stop, 2 for a bad option or combination of options, 1 when
the server can't start (a port is taken, the database can't be opened, or a
certificate the configuration names can't be served).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR firewall\-cmd (1),
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR update\-crypto\-policies (8),
.BR semanage\-port (8)
.PP
The API: /usr/share/doc/houstonkvm/API.md.
